{
  "item_id": "PRISMA_2020_3",
  "elements": [
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_3_e1",
      "status": "PRESENT",
      "chunk_id": "text_2",
      "quote": "Foot and ankle fractures are common lower extremity injuries, with ankle fractures comprising 17% of all fractures requiring hospitalisation [1]. The incidence of foot fractures has been reported as 142.3/100,000 person-years and the overall incidence of foot and ankle fractures combined has been calculated to be 25.87/10,000 person-years [2, 3]. ... Such 2D renderings, however, fail to accurately depict the structural complexities of bony fractures, which may lead to imperfect reconstruction. This is problematic in the context of foot and ankle fractures, as improper reduction can lead to post-traumatic osteoarthritis of articular surfaces, which in turn can greatly impair mobility [4, 10]. 3D printing, also known as additive manufacturing or rapid prototyping, is a rapidly expanding technology that is beginning to revolutionise the medical and healthcare industry [11, 12]. ... In this context, the 3D printed models facilitate surgery by enabling an enhanced understanding of the anatomy, improved communication between clinicians and patients, and improved tailoring of both tools and surgical techniques to the individual fracture [19]; as such, this new approach constitutes a form of personalised medicine, which is central to contemporary healthcare.",
      "reason": "The introduction describes epidemiology, limitations of current 2D planning, and how 3D printing may address these uncertainties—i.e., current state of knowledge and important uncertainties."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_3_e2",
      "status": "PRESENT",
      "chunk_id": "text_1",
      "quote": "Purpose The systematic review aims to establish the value of using 3D printing-assisted pre-operative planning, compared to conventional planning, for the operative management of foot and ankle fractures.",
      "reason": "The abstract explicitly states the aim to establish the value/need for reviewing 3D printing-assisted pre-operative planning versus conventional planning, framed by the clinical problem described in the introduction."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_3_e3",
      "status": "MISSING",
      "chunk_id": null,
      "quote": null,
      "reason": "No text in the provided evidence explains whether similar systematic reviews exist or cites/justifies the need relative to prior reviews."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_3_e4",
      "status": "PRESENT",
      "chunk_id": "text_2",
      "quote": "In this context, the 3D printed models facilitate surgery by enabling an enhanced understanding of the anatomy, improved communication between clinicians and patients, and improved tailoring of both tools and surgical techniques to the individual fracture [19]; as such, this new approach constitutes a form of personalised medicine, which is central to contemporary healthcare.",
      "reason": "The introduction briefly describes the proposed mechanism by which 3D printed models improve surgical care (enhanced anatomical understanding, communication, and tailoring of tools/technique)."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_3_e5",
      "status": "MISSING",
      "chunk_id": null,
      "quote": null,
      "reason": "No evidence was provided of a logic model or diagram outlining hypothesised relationships between intervention components and outcomes."
    }
  ],
  "confidence": 75
